"""The Necrobinder's strategy, shared by planning and individual decisions.

Owner's guidance: card removals and a smaller deck, Block and preserving Osty's HP, while
looking for scaling.

The numbers in the text are what the highest-rated Spire Codex players did with the Necrobinder on
the pinned game version: 137 solo A10 standard runs of builds v0.107.1 and v0.107.0 by the 12
highest-rated accounts that have them, 104 won, read 2026-11-25 with ``python -m sts_agent.pilot players
--character NECROBINDER --words Doom,Osty`` (the decks carried into each act boss and what was taken at
2401 card rewards, counted by ``evidence.deck_balance``'s role rules). The build names come from the
Spire Codex guides (builds guide, Necrobinder tier list, "Offensive Necrobinder" and "Necrobinder's True
Potential"), which describe other patches.
"""

PROMPT_VERSION = "necrobinder-strategic-planner-prompt-3"

DECK_GUIDANCE = """\
THE NECROBINDER'S DECK. The deck stays smaller than the other characters' by removing cards and by refusing attacks, not by refusing everything. In 137 solo A10 runs on this game version by the highest-rated Spire Codex players (104 won), the deck entering the act 1, 2 and 3 bosses held about 19, 25 and 32 cards after four removals a run, and they took 84% of the card rewards in act 1, 65% in act 2 and 54% in act 3. They followed no size limit: with 24 or more cards in act 2 they still took six rewards in ten, as with fewer, and in simulated runs a deck held to 18 cards from the first picks lost the act 1 boss more often. Removals come before additions from act 2 on, where those players made half of theirs: at a shop buy the removal before any card. In act 1 the shop's gold first goes to what wins the act 1 fights, and a removal is bought with what is left (in simulated runs buying it first in act 1 lost the act 1 boss more often). Prefer events and relics that remove or transform; remove starter Strikes first (four in five of those players' act 1 removals, half later), then curses, and a Defend only late, because this deck's damage comes from Osty, Doom and a few multiplied attacks while its plain Block is thin.

Block and Osty's HP are one defensive budget, and Weak protects both. While Osty is alive he absorbs all unblocked attack damage, so his HP is Block that lasts between turns, and it is also his damage; an enemy that is Weak or has lost Strength takes less from both. Cards that apply Weak or lower enemy Strength were what those players took most: four times in five with none in the deck and still more than half the time with four. The deck_balance track "weak" asks for three, four in a deck over 24 cards and five over 32; while it is short such a card comes first, ahead of plain Block, and one that also gains Block is the best of them. A Block card was taken nearly half the time as the first or second and three times in ten after, so Block is never marked over-supplied. A large Summon was taken three times in four, the cheap Summon cards one time in five at any count: the starter's Summon and one large one keep Osty up, and Summon is not added for its own sake. A card that kills Osty for a one-time effect (the track "osty") gives up the HP the Summon cards built and the attacks that use it; take one only with a named way to get the fight finished by it, and never a second.

What those players refused is attacks: an attack that only deals damage one time in six in act 1 and one in fifteen after, and a damage card of any kind one in four for the first two and one in eight after; deck_balance marks damage over-supplied once the deck holds more damage cards than those decks did (from the fifth in act 1, the seventh in act 2, the ninth after). Doom cards and Osty's attacks are the exception once the deck is built on them: both were taken more often the more of them the deck held (one in six or fewer with none, one in three with three or four), so add them to a route that exists and not as a second route. Energy, draw and other utility were taken a third of the time or more in every act: keep adding them. Scaling, because flat damage does not end act 2 and act 3 bosses: a damage-scaling Power (one that multiplies attack damage or what Vulnerable adds, most often) was taken four times in ten as the first source and three in ten as the second; the community guides name three engines, Souls with their Ethereal payoffs, Osty, and Doom (an enemy dies at the end of its turn once its Doom reaches its HP), of which two together win, and a small offensive build of Weak-and-Block cards with damage multipliers and cards that return a card from the discard pile to replay it. One route that the deck draws every cycle beats two half-built ones."""

ELITE_GUIDANCE = """\
THE NECROBINDER'S ELITE ROUTES. The Necrobinder has the lowest HP of the five and no healing after fights, so HP spent on the route is not coming back before the next rest site, and elites are where it goes. Two measurements bound the judgment. In simulated A10 runs a strong drafter lost about one elite in six in act 1 and one in three in act 2 with this character, against one in twenty-five and one in eleven with the Ironclad, and an elite it beat cost a third of max HP or more. The highest-rated players take them all the same: 2.2 elites in act 1, 2.0 in act 2 and 1.7 in act 3 a run, losing 14%, 20% and 12% of max HP to each, and they enter the act 1 boss at 60% HP. What their deck holds by the act 1 boss is about nine added cards on three starter Strikes: one or two cards that apply Weak, three Block cards, three or four that deal damage, one scaling piece and one or two energy cards. So elites are taken selectively in every act, and act 1 can plan none when the deck, HP or recovery cannot support one. Take act 1 elites when the deck is on that pace and Osty's HP and its Block can absorb the elite's big turns: a route with a rest site before or right behind the elite, and Weak or Block picked up in the rooms before it. An elite with several enemies or summons needs an attack that hits ALL enemies or Doom on ALL enemies first. From act 2 on prefer routes that pass a shop, because the removal is then this deck's best purchase, and count it when comparing a shop route with an extra elite. In later acts plan at most one elite, behind a rest site, while Block scaling or damage scaling is still missing; a second with HP to spare and a rest site between them. State which elites are accepted, where the shop and rest sites fall, and what would remove or add one."""
